					that people wish it. Every one of these trinkets is a tombstone, a memorial to a war so brutal that the mere
					act of surviving it made you a hero. Every bloodstained scrap of iron that lies behind glass or locked in a
					safe is all that remains of thousands of soldiers who died in the mud so that a few lucky souls could be
					hailed as legends. People look at our relics and speak of courage and glory, but not a one of them was worth
					the price we paid in people. Can you imagine what we could have had, if they'd lived instead? The art, the
					progress, the families they would have made?</q>
			</strong></blockquote>
